-<p>BLS counted 340.7 thousand seasonally adjusted nonfarm jobs in Alaska in September 2026, preliminary, and printed a change of +1.2% over twelve months [^17], and a seasonally adjusted civilian labor force of 365.2 thousand [^5]. The Department of Labor and Workforce Development's own release for the same month reports jobs up <span data-fsc-nocount>0.1%</span> from September 2025 [^18]. The two are different series from different publishers, each with its own basis, and a study that needs a statewide job count picks one, names it and does not average or reconcile them.</p>
-<p>The sector lines say more about a given project than the total does. In BLS's seasonally adjusted September 2026 figures, all preliminary, construction held 17.7 thousand jobs (-5.3%), government 76.8 thousand (-3.0%) and manufacturing 13.9 thousand (+14.9%), each change measured over twelve months as printed [^5]. The department's Alaska Economic Trends for August 2026 carries an article on the federal jobs Alaska lost in 2025 [^19], and federal employers sit inside the government line.</p>
-<p>Each line points at different property types. The government count is the one a project in Juneau, or one leasing to agencies in Anchorage or Fairbanks, cites for its tenant base. The construction count is both a demand signal for builders' supply yards and the labor pool a project's own contractor hires from, so a schedule for a 2027 start is written with it in view. The manufacturing line takes in seafood processing along with other plants, and a processing or industrial study looks past the statewide change to the employer and the borough before using it.</p>
-<p>Business Employment Dynamics adds the churn behind the totals. From September to December 2025, Alaska's private sector recorded 30,293 gross job gains at opening and expanding establishments and 23,914 gross job losses at closing and contracting ones, seasonally adjusted, for a net change of +6,379 [^20]. For a start-up borrower the gains and losses are the more useful pair: they show that openings and closings both run in the thousands of jobs within a single quarter, which is the setting a new business's survival assumptions have to fit.</p>
-<p>Output is reported in two measures. In BEA's annual figures, as FRED carries them, Alaska's all-industry GDP was 73,634.8 million dollars in 2025 in current dollars [^21] and 56,335.0 million chained 2017 dollars in real terms [^22]. The quarterly real series, at a seasonally adjusted annual rate, reads 56,342.4 million chained 2017 dollars for the second quarter of 2025 and 56,159.6 million chained 2017 dollars for the second quarter of 2026 [^23]. A study prints both quarters as BEA published them and states which one its argument uses.</p>
